"""Original recreation and inclusion drill; first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
Leisure generally refers to|time free from required work or obligations|only the time spent in compulsory work|only participation in organised competition|only the activity selected during free time|Leisure commonly means available free time.
Recreation commonly refers to|activities chosen for enjoyment or restoration|only compulsory employment|only clinical treatment|only competitive fixtures|Recreation involves chosen activities for enjoyment or restoration.
Reading for enjoyment during free time can be|recreation|a compulsory task regardless of the reader’s choice|a form of exercise in every case|a competitive sport by definition|Recreation can include quiet activities such as reading.
Walking for enjoyment in free time can be|recreation and physical activity|exercise only and never physical activity|competitive sport in every case|neither recreation nor physical activity|A chosen walk can be both recreation and physical activity.
Recreation can be|active or relatively quiet|limited to organised competition|limited to outdoor activities|available only to elite athletes|Recreation can take many forms and settings.
A board game chosen for enjoyment can be|recreation|a compulsory duty in every case|necessarily physical exercise|a competitive sport in every case|A board game can be a chosen recreational activity.
Leisure and recreation differ because|one generally describes free time and the other a chosen activity|both always refer only to competitive sport|recreation refers only to available time|leisure means a chosen game and recreation means free time|Leisure concerns time; recreation concerns chosen activity.
A person may experience the same activity differently from another person because|interests and circumstances vary|every activity produces identical experiences|preferences never affect experience|everyone has the same recreational goals|Recreational experiences vary by person and context.
Recreation can support|enjoyment and social connection|guaranteed cure of every condition|removal of all safety requirements|identical benefits for everyone|Recreation may support enjoyment and connection, though effects vary.
A recreational activity should not be described as|a guaranteed treatment for every health condition|a possible source of enjoyment|a possible social activity|a chosen use of free time|Recreation is not a guaranteed treatment and effects depend on context.
Access to recreation can be affected by|cost, transport, facilities and safety|motivation alone in all circumstances|skill alone regardless of facilities|individual preferences without any environmental influence|Resources and environment can influence access.
Dance commonly combines|movement and rhythm|a fixed score in every performance|a single movement pattern shared by all cultures|competition without any movement|Dance involves movement and rhythm in varied contexts.
Dance can have|cultural, social, recreational or performance purposes|only one universal purpose|no cultural or social significance|only a medical purpose|Dance has varied purposes depending on context.
Different dance traditions should be approached with|respect for their cultural context|assuming all styles have identical meaning|ignoring participants’ experiences|disregarding the cultural setting|Respectful learning recognises differing contexts and traditions.
Planning a dance session should consider|space, footwear, intensity and participants' needs|only the organiser’s personal preference|intensity without considering the space|the activity name without checking the setting|The environment and individual needs matter to safety.
An injured participant in an activity should|stop and seek appropriate assistance|always continue through pain|be diagnosed from the name of the activity|be ignored unless the session ends|Injury and concerning symptoms should be taken seriously.
Adapted physical activity modifies|a task, equipment or environment to support participation|a participant’s medical diagnosis|only the title of the activity|all participants’ bodies in the same way|Adaptations can modify activities or settings to support participation.
An adaptation should be chosen with attention to|the individual, goal, setting and safety|a diagnostic label without consulting the individual|the final score regardless of access|a fixed adjustment without reviewing the setting|Appropriate adjustments depend on the person and context.
The participant's preferences|should be considered in planning an adaptation|never affect the choice of adaptation|must match all other participants’ preferences|replace every safety consideration|Involving the participant helps identify meaningful adjustments.
Two participants sharing a disability label|can have different abilities or preferences|must have identical needs|always require exclusion|cannot have preferences relevant to activity|People with the same label can have different needs and goals.
Changing a game's equipment after discussion with a learner can be|an appropriate adaptation|a guarantee of injury-free activity|automatic exclusion from the game|a clinical diagnosis|Equipment changes can support participation when suitable.
Changing the size of an activity space may be|an environmental adaptation|a universal treatment for injury|a change to the participant’s diagnosis|an adjustment that never affects access|Changing space can adapt the environment to the activity and participants.
Changing how instructions are communicated may be|an adaptation to support participation|a change of medical diagnosis|automatic exclusion from the activity|a substitute for reviewing safety|Communication adjustments can help participants understand a task.
An adapted game can|preserve its goal while changing how it is performed|must discard every aspect of the original goal|automatically removes all risk|needs no review after implementation|Adaptations can preserve an activity's objective while changing its form.
The effect of an adaptation should be|reviewed with attention to participation and safety|assumed to have the same effect for everyone|ignored once implemented|treated as a medical diagnosis|Review helps determine whether an adjustment works for the participant.
A larger ball may help a particular learner, but|it is not automatically the right choice for everyone|must help all participants in exactly the same way|replaces communication with the learner|guarantees that no injury can occur|The suitable adjustment varies by person and task.
Adaptation is different from exclusion because|it aims to support meaningful participation|it always prevents participation|it ignores the individual’s preferences|it assumes everyone has identical abilities|Appropriate adaptation supports meaningful participation.
When planning a recreation session, an organiser should consider|purpose, people, equipment, environment and supervision|the final score without considering the environment|the activity name without checking equipment|one participant’s clothing alone|Planning should consider the people, task, environment and supervision.
Inspecting an activity area helps|identify hazards|guarantee that no incident is possible|remove the need for instructions|replace appropriate supervision|Inspection helps identify hazards but does not eliminate every risk.
Clear instructions help participants|understand the activity and how to report a problem|prevent every injury automatically|remove the need for supervision|change the participant’s diagnosis|Clear communication supports understanding and safety.
A participant should know|how to report a problem and obtain assistance|that concerns should be hidden|that all pain must be ignored|that assistance should never be requested|Participants need a way to communicate problems and obtain help.
An exercise for rehabilitation should be selected|with appropriate qualified guidance for the individual|solely from a diagnostic label without consultation|without considering symptoms or circumstances|by copying any other person’s programme|Rehabilitation needs individualised qualified guidance.
A person can choose quiet recreation such as|reading|compulsory work chosen by an employer|a mandatory emergency procedure|an activity that must be physically vigorous|Reading can be a quiet recreational activity.
A physical recreation example is|a walk chosen for enjoyment|a compulsory task involving no bodily movement|reading while seated without movement|a medical consultation as such|Walking for enjoyment can be physical recreation.
Enjoyment during recreation|can vary between participants|must be identical for everyone|is irrelevant to recreation|establishes a medical diagnosis|People's preferences and experiences vary.
A social benefit that recreation may support is|connection with other people|guaranteed prevention of all disease|elimination of every access barrier|replacement of clinical care|Recreation can offer opportunities for social connection.
Which statement about dance is careful?|styles and purposes vary across contexts|every dance has the same meaning|dance has no social purpose in any context|all dance is competitive by definition|Dance traditions and purposes vary.
Preparation and rest in an activity|can support participation but do not guarantee zero risk|remove every possible hazard|replace individual advice in every situation|are unnecessary for every participant|Preparation and recovery support participation without eliminating all risk.
The first step in a useful inclusion cycle is to|ask about the participant's goals and preferences|assume identical needs without consultation|exclude the participant before considering the task|ignore the participant’s safety|Involving the participant helps guide appropriate adjustments.
After trying an adjustment, the organiser should|review participation and safety|assume the adjustment works equally for everyone|ignore the participant’s feedback|treat the adjustment itself as a diagnosis|Review and feedback help assess whether the adaptation is suitable.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
